WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.760
Put 10 molecules of DNA in a tube.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.360
30 rounds of copying can turn them into about 10 billion.

3
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.900
No living cell is involved.

4
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.860
How does a tube of DNA do that?

5
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.880
And why does a real reaction stop short?

6
00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:23.533
The trick is two short primers.

7
00:00:24.253 --> 00:00:28.673
A primer pairs with the template and gives the polymerase a place to start.

8
00:00:29.593 --> 00:00:34.693
The two primers bind opposite strands, one on each side of the region to copy.

9
00:00:35.893 --> 00:00:39.213
Polymerase can only add to the 3' end of a primer.

10
00:00:39.693 --> 00:00:44.693
So the primers must point toward each other, 3' ends facing in.

11
00:00:45.517 --> 00:00:47.777
Each cycle has three steps.

12
00:00:48.637 --> 00:00:54.317
First, heat the tube to about 94 to 98 °C, and the two strands

13
00:00:54.317 --> 00:00:59.637
separate. Second, cool the tube a few degrees below the primers' melting

14
00:00:59.637 --> 00:01:02.337
temperature. Each primer pairs with its site.

99
00:01:03.477 --> 00:01:10.437
Third, typically at 72 °C for Taq polymerase, each primer is lengthened from 5' to 3'.

17
00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:16.707
In cycle 1, copies start at a primer but run past the other site.

18
00:01:17.527 --> 00:01:22.427
Because the primers face each other, each new strand contains a site for the other

19
00:01:22.427 --> 00:01:26.947
primer. So the products of one cycle become templates for the next.

20
00:01:27.827 --> 00:01:30.907
It is like a photocopier whose copies can be copied.

21
00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:36.167
Molecules bounded by both primers first appear in cycle 3.

22
00:01:36.887 --> 00:01:40.627
After three cycles, two of the eight are exact length.

23
00:01:41.627 --> 00:01:44.547
By cycle 30, they are nearly all of the product.

24
00:01:45.417 --> 00:01:48.097
Doubling is what makes this powerful.

25
00:01:48.997 --> 00:01:54.237
With perfect doubling, each cycle multiplies the number of copies by 2.

26
00:01:55.237 --> 00:02:00.997
Thirty cycles multiply it by 2 to the thirtieth power, which is just over one

27
00:02:00.997 --> 00:02:05.457
billion. Ten molecules become about ten billion.

28
00:02:06.367 --> 00:02:08.667
A real reaction stops short.

29
00:02:09.387 --> 00:02:13.227
Amplification is exponential only in the early cycles.

30
00:02:13.807 --> 00:02:16.187
Then it slows and reaches a plateau.

31
00:02:17.287 --> 00:02:22.107
In one model system, reactions with different starting amounts reached the same

32
00:02:22.107 --> 00:02:28.107
final level. So a bright band at the end does not tell you how much DNA you began

33
00:02:28.107 --> 00:02:32.607
with. Each cycle doubles the copies, but only early on.
